      <section id="s2">
        <h2>#2 What is sexuality?</h2>
        <div class="blog">
          <p>When it comes to sexuality, the first question is: What is it about? It can be about
            sexual identities, sexual desires, sexual behaviors... However, all these things 
            appear to be private matters for individuals. There seems to be an invisible force that makes sexuality seem asocial,
            static, and unchangeable.</p>
          <p>

            To demystify sexuality, we have to understand the social construction of sexuality, which can be examined through three contemporary approaches: queer theory, intersectionality, and political economy.</p>
          <p>

            Actually, I was quite confused when I first heard about social construction. It seemed unrelated to sexuality, more like a term from sociology. However, socially constructed does not mean imaginary: these constructions are real and can change.</p>
          <p>

            Sexuality is socially constructed, which means it changes with its social context. Looking at ideas about the purpose of sex, we can trace one historical perspective back to Puritanism, which treated sex outside heterosexual marriage as sinful and emphasized reproduction.
            During industrialization, ideas about sex and love changed alongside attitudes toward children and family life. In the nineteenth century, Victorian ideas associated love with femininity and sex with masculinity, distinctions that remain influential today. In the 1920s, ideas about sex and pleasure gained visibility alongside an economic boom. Entertainment such as nickelodeons and burlesque shows became popular, and socializing across gender became more common. Later, ideas of sexual egalitarianism and freedom gained prominence during the sexual revolution and second-wave feminism of the 1960s.</p>
          <p>

            This brief history shows how understandings of sexuality evolve in economic, historical, and political contexts. Sexuality is a socially constituted historical practice. Other aspects of how people understand reality—including gender, race, and concepts such as family—are also shaped through shared understandings, language, history, and power relations.</p>
