      <section id="s3">
        <h2>#3 Intersectionality</h2>
        <div class="blog">
          <p>How many words would you need to fully describe yourself? It may be hard to answer, but there are many ways to talk about how you experience the world and identify yourself: your gender, race, citizenship, native language, work, and where you live, among others. Sociologists recognize these as social identities, and they can carry substantial personal significance. Our cultures, societies, and interactions give them meaning. They can also shape our experiences of privilege and oppression.</p>
          <p>In 1976, Emma DeGraffenreid and several other Black women sued General Motors for discrimination. They argued that the company segregated its workforce by race and gender: jobs available to Black workers were given to men, while jobs available to women were given to white women. The court dismissed their claims, refusing to let them combine race and gender into a single claim. As a result, their experiences fell through the cracks. This case helps illustrate intersectionality: race and gender can interact to shape experiences of discrimination that cannot be fully understood by examining either in isolation (Crenshaw, 2015).</p>
          <p>Intersectionality is a way of thinking about identity and its relationship to power. It can also help us understand the complexity of sexuality. Sexuality does not exist in isolation; it intersects with other aspects of identity. Together, these intersections shape our experiences of privilege, oppression, and power.</p>
          <div class="references">
            <h3>Reference</h3>
            <p>Crenshaw, K. (2015, September 24). <a href="https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/in-theory/wp/2015/09/24/why-intersectionality-cant-wait/">Why intersectionality can’t wait</a>. <em>The Washington Post</em>.</p>
          </div>
