      <section id="s4">
        <h2>#4 Sex &amp; gender</h2>
        <div class="blog">
          <figure class="article-figure">
            <img src="/img/other/louis%20XIV.png" alt="A portrait of Louis XIV wearing high-heeled shoes">
            <figcaption>Louis XIV wearing high-heeled shoes.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>Look at this portrait of Louis XIV wearing high heels. It may seem surprising that high heels worn by men once represented power and status, while today they are often associated with formal dress for women. Imagine a man wearing heels to show off his power today! This contrast suggests how people align themselves with groups and distinguish themselves from others. This is not always a bad thing: belonging to a group can bring us a sense of connection and joy. But differentiation can also become a form of classification through which power and privilege are distributed unevenly.</p>
          <p>Sex is often used as a way to categorize people based on biological characteristics. We are born with primary sex characteristics, and secondary sex characteristics develop as we grow up. Sex is commonly treated as binary, although biological variation does not always fit neatly into two categories. Gender, on the other hand, concerns social meanings, including the ideas of masculinity and femininity that we attach to bodies. Fausto-Sterling’s work examines the relationship between sex and gender. Many beliefs about men and women do not simply arise from differences between male and female bodies; they are also produced and maintained through active, interactive, and cooperative social processes.</p>
          <p>The common assumption that male-bodied people are masculine and female-bodied people are feminine treats gender as if it follows directly from sex. This assumption is part of an ideology often called the gender binary, but it does not capture everyone’s experience. In parts of early modern Europe, some scholars described male and female bodies through a one-sex model; Renaissance thinkers, for example, described female genitalia as an internal version of male genitalia. Biological differences do not make bodies opposites: we are not all the same, but we are not simply opposites either.</p>
          <p>Nevertheless, most of us encounter two gender categories in everyday life, and binary thinking remains influential. We hear phrases such as “real men don’t cry” and “women are emotional.” Categories such as “men” and “women” can still say something meaningful about a person. But do any of us fit perfectly into a binary? We often speak and act as if gender stereotypes are true, even when they do not fully describe us. How much of what we take for granted is socially constructed?</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section id="s5">
        <h2>#5 Compulsory heterosexuality &amp; heteronormative sexual scripts</h2>
        <div class="blog">
          <p>The gender binary discussed above presents the idea that there are only two kinds of people: female-bodied people who are feminine and male-bodied people who are masculine, with gender determined by biological sex. It is also linked to assumptions about sexual orientation, including the expectation that men are attracted to women and women to men. Compulsory heterosexuality describes the social expectation that heterosexuality is natural or inevitable. A related concept, heteronormativity, refers to the assumption that heterosexuality is the norm. We encounter these ideas from childhood-for example, in Disney stories where a beautiful princess finds her true love-a prince. Or think about a someone asking a girl, “Do you have a boyfriend?”</p>
          <p>In everyday life, we also hear about “gaydar,” the supposed ability to identify someone’s sexual orientation. It often relies on stereotypes, such as the ideas that gay men are feminine and lesbians are masculine. People may assume that a man is gay because of the way he dresses or speaks. What they are observing is his gender expression, not his sexual orientation. These assumptions confuse the two: not everyone who departs from traditional gender norms is gay or lesbian. Homophobia is also closely linked to gender policing and social expectations about masculinity and femininity.</p>
          <p>Gendered expectations are deeply involved in heteronormative sexual scripts. When we examine heterosexuality, we are not only considering the sex of the people involved in attraction or sexual encounters; we can also ask how they express or perform gender in those contexts.</p>
          <p>A sexual script is a set of social expectations that can guide sexual interactions. The word “script” suggests that people may follow familiar patterns rather than approaching every encounter without preconceptions. Think about a date: who is expected to ask the other person out, pay for the meal, or make the first move? In a heterosexual relationship, we may assume that the man will take the lead and the woman will wait for him. These expectations can influence how relationships unfold, how boundaries are communicated, and how consent is understood. They can also reflect and reproduce differences in power and inequality. </p>
        </div>
